WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:00.953
- Welcome back.

00:00:00.953 --> 00:00:02.310
We've got the one and only,

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.832
Carol Burnett with us
on her 90th birthday.

00:00:04.832 --> 00:00:06.200
(audience applauds)

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.033
- You can see her tribute,

00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:09.420
"Carol Burnett: 90 Years
of Laughter and Love"

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.860
tonight on NBC.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.120
Right now, we're actually gonna go

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.560
through Carol's scrapbooks.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.690
So, let's flip to some other memories

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.900
from "The Carol Burnett Show",
the movie parody she did.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.390
So, one of Carol's favorite
parodies was called,

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.497
Lovely Story, based on the famous film

00:00:23.497 --> 00:00:25.080
"Love Story" from 1970.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.630
So, let's see a little of that.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.040
- What is it, doc?

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.709
- I'm afraid she only
has five minutes to live.

00:00:31.709 --> 00:00:33.469
(audience laughs)
(dramatic music)

00:00:33.469 --> 00:00:34.920
- Oh, no.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.540
Oh, no.
(actor cries)

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.043
Five minutes to live.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.043
She's so young, so pretty.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.383
It's not fair.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.833
Oh, doctor, what can I do?

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.840
- Well, first of all, don't overact.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.673
(audience laughs)

00:00:55.669 --> 00:00:57.780
- I know, I know.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.080
I'll act as though nothing had happened.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.100
(actor sniffs)

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.220
Hey, funny face.
(audience laughs)

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:06.330
Guess what?
- What?

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.590
- The doctor says you're
gonna be all right.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.000
There's nothing wrong.
- Oh.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.320
- How about that?

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.630
Hey, how'd you like something to eat?

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.395
- I'd love a four minute egg.

00:01:14.395 --> 00:01:17.757
(audience laughs)

00:01:17.757 --> 00:01:19.300
- It holds up.
(audience applauds)

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.080
It's like, it holds up, oh, my God.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.040
What do you remember about that?

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.530
- That's, that's one of
the funniest lines ever.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:26.820
- Oh my gosh, it's hilarious.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.340
- I'd love a four minute...
I, oh, that was great.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:32.670
And I got a phone call from Ali MacGraw,

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:33.800
who was...
- In the original.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:34.800
- In the movie.
- Yeah.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.540
- She loved it.
- Yeah.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.900
- She absolutely, she said,
it was a, it was hysterical.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:40.733
- [Kelly] Oh, my God.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:42.224
- Ryan O'Neal wasn't thrilled.

00:01:42.224 --> 00:01:43.110
- Yeah.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:43.943
- No, he didn't like...
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:43.943 --> 00:01:45.880
- Right, because the overacting.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:46.713
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:46.713 --> 00:01:48.030
- Probably.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:49.920
- Once "The Carol Burnett Show" ended,

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.290
Carol moved on to other
TV and film projects

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.909
including playing Miss
Hannigan in the movie "Annie".

00:01:54.909 --> 00:01:57.090
(audience applauds)

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.730
So, Miss Hannigan was pretty scary.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:00.563
- Yeah.

00:02:00.563 --> 00:02:02.100
- [Kelly] I feel like, how did kids react?

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.870
Like did they, did they in real life

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:05.070
think maybe you were mean?

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.270
(Carol laughs)

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.000
- Sometimes, it depends
on how old they are.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.190
I might be in a restaurant and,

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.870
because now they're watching it, you know,

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.580
they, they bought the...
- Yeah.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.870
- DVD and everything.
- Yeah.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.030
- So, it's a lot of, lot of little kids

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:19.110
are still watching it.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:20.850
And it depends on how old they are,

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:22.500
if they're really young,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.760
they kind of slink back a
little bit if they recognize me.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:26.593
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:26.593 --> 00:02:29.231
- And then, I always say,
"I was just pretending".

00:02:29.231 --> 00:02:30.089
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:30.089 --> 00:02:31.050
- Yes, really not her.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:31.883
- [Carol] Not her at all.

00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:33.840
- I'm a really good actor.
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.100
- But then, when they get older,

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.930
they, they're, like, kind of
excited to meet Miss Hannigan.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:40.763
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:40.763 --> 00:02:42.750
- And I said, "I really
do love little girls".

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:43.710
And I'm not, you know.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.180
- It's such an amazing
thing right, though?

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.328
To be a part of people's
childhood like that.

00:02:47.328 --> 00:02:48.161
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:48.161 --> 00:02:51.120
- Like, it's just, it's,
there's very few films

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.231
like "Annie", "Sound of Music", like...

00:02:53.231 --> 00:02:54.064
- [Carol] Right.

00:02:54.064 --> 00:02:55.680
- Just certain things you watch
when you're a kid growing up

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.450
and then, you meet, that's, it's a,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:01.410
not everybody has that weight
in pop culture, I don't think.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.980
- [Carol] Well, what was
wonderful too, in filming it

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.810
the little orphans that, you know,

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:10.200
the little kids who played
the orphans were all adorable.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.190
They were, yeah.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.260
There wasn't one brat in the pack.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.093
- [Kelly] Really?
(audience laughs)

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:14.926
- [Carol] No.

00:03:14.926 --> 00:03:16.980
And of course, Aileen
Quinn, who played Annie.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:17.813
- Yeah.

00:03:17.813 --> 00:03:19.530
- You know, she's a grown up lady now.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:20.363
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:20.363 --> 00:03:21.660
- And she's on the special that we did.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:22.710
- I know.
- Yeah.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.420
- Coming out and singing,
that was incredible.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:25.253
- [Carol] Yeah.

00:03:25.253 --> 00:03:27.270
- Well, moving on to the mid 1980s,

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.160
we've gotta keep things
moving, it's a lot of years.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.160
So, and the 1990s, Carol had serious roles

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.200
in TV, movies like "Friendly Fire",

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.360
dramatic roles in shows like "Magnum P.I."

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.730
and acted in sitcoms like "Mad About You".

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:40.110
I loved all of those.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.940
So, you've had so many different roles.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.370
So, one of your most
dramatic was "Friendly Fire".

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.140
So, how did that come about?

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.220
Did you wanna go dramatic?

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:48.630
Was this something that you wanted to do?

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.679
- Well, I didn't shy away from it.

00:03:50.679 --> 00:03:51.512
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:51.512 --> 00:03:53.430
- But nobody really ever
thought of me to do that.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:54.263
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:54.263 --> 00:03:55.440
- And I remember when I got the script,

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.150
I kept looking, did they
send, it did have my name on,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.770
on the envelope, you know?

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:05.490
And what happened was that
the director was British,

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:08.733
so he didn't know my work
that much on the show and,

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.090
but he saw me do the last show

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:16.080
when I said goodnight and
goodbye for the 11 years,

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.720
where I'm sitting on the
bucket as a chairwoman

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.430
and I'm talking about and,

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:23.580
and I did get a little
teary-eyed and stuff like that.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.525
And he said, "I think I want
her to read the script".

00:04:26.525 --> 00:04:27.358
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:27.358 --> 00:04:28.191
- That was that.

00:04:28.191 --> 00:04:29.024
- I'm telling you, the death...

00:04:29.024 --> 00:04:30.540
- Out of the blue.
- Yeah.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:31.373
- Yeah.

00:04:31.373 --> 00:04:33.000
- That's just from that one
moment, isn't that crazy?

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:33.833
- [Carol] Yeah.

00:04:33.833 --> 00:04:34.890
- What's even crazier
is that you were like,

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:36.390
he wasn't familiar with your work.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.670
That's, that's even crazier to me.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:41.390
I'm like, "Well, I've just
been here for a minute".

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:43.500
A, a scrapbook wouldn't be a scrapbook

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.020
if we didn't have a page dedicated

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:47.820
to all the awards that you have won.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.093
So, in addition to many
entertainment awards,

00:04:50.093 --> 00:04:51.210
(audience applauds)

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:54.000
Carol is a Kennedy Center
Honors Presidential Medal

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.860
of Freedom and the Mark Twain Prize

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.120
for American Humor recipient.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.910
So, of all the honors you've been given,

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.070
was there one that surprised you the most?

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:03.390
That you were like, "Wow".

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.760
- Well, the one that
surprised me was the, uh,

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.250
surprise, was the recent one,

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.520
The Golden Globe for
Television Performance.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:12.353
But...
- Yeah.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.690
- They made it a, like they had

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.360
the Cecil B. Demille Award for movies,

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.314
they made it the Carol
Burnett Award for television.

00:05:21.314 --> 00:05:22.147
(audience applauds)

00:05:22.147 --> 00:05:23.866
- [Kelly] Incredible.

00:05:23.866 --> 00:05:27.660
- But my, but my, I guess
my favorite award...

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:28.714
- I cry a lot on this show.
(Kelly laughs)

00:05:28.714 --> 00:05:31.260
- Thank you, my favorite...
- They've always got a stock.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:34.650
- My favorite one, was the
very first one I ever got.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:38.694
And that was at UCLA in 1952.
- Yeah.

00:05:38.694 --> 00:05:41.400
- I was the most outstanding newcomer

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.110
in the theater arts department.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:43.943
- Oh, my God.
(audience applauds)

00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:47.280
- So, I still have that, yeah.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.050
- That's so funny, because still the,

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.640
the thing that I thought, I mean,

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.280
I've won awards, but like, I
remember even in my yearbook,

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:54.240
like in high school, they've got,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.940
I got like most talented and
I was like, "Wait, what?"

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:58.800
Like, there were so many talented people

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.240
that I went to school with and I was like,

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.310
I, it's so funny what sticks out to you.

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:03.840
Like, you win Grammys, you win things,

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:05.554
but it's like those...
- First ones.

00:06:05.554 --> 00:06:07.230
- Because I think those
first ones kind of,

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:09.330
really do push you to keep going and like,

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:10.320
give you confidence.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:11.153
- Right, absolutely.

00:06:11.153 --> 00:06:14.070
- Yeah, we talked about
how hard it was for a woman

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:17.250
in entertainment during the
earlier parts of Carol's career.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:18.900
So, so many of the biggest women

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:20.670
in comedy say you paved the way for them,

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.260
because that's true.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.330
Like Maya Rudolph, Amy
Poehler, Jane Lynch,

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.490
all those women, hilarious
and I love to death.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.890
So, what does that mean to
you when people say that?

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:30.570
That you've kind of pioneered the way and,

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:32.313
and opened up opportunity?

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.440
- You know, what I say is,
if I had never been born,

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.525
they would be doing what they're doing.

00:06:39.525 --> 00:06:42.330
- I, I mean, agreed in a certain sense.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:43.380
- [Carol] Talent will out.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:47.070
- But not everybody can,
not everybody, you know,

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.440
is told, "Oh, this is, this is, you know,

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.870
the gals do something else,
this is what the men do".

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:54.360
Not every, some people, you know,

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:57.030
get that hurdle and then
they, it becomes a stop sign.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:57.863
- [Carol] Yeah.

00:06:57.863 --> 00:06:59.340
- And some people look at it as a hurdle

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.470
and they, they're like,
"Okay, I'm gonna persevere".

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:03.240
So, not everybody does that.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:05.340
What, why do you think you were that kid?

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.320
That was like, "No, I'm
gonna plow through it".

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:08.610
I feel like I'm that kid that I'm like,

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:10.956
you tell me you can't and it
makes me wanna show you I can.

00:07:10.956 --> 00:07:13.020
- I, I don't know.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:16.320
I just, I, I was secure
in what I was doing.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:20.250
But then also, sometimes,

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.980
a lot of times, I get a fan
mail or whatever that says,

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:25.410
how, how do you deal with rejection?

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:26.243
- Yeah.

00:07:26.243 --> 00:07:30.125
- You know, and I remember I was up for,

00:07:30.125 --> 00:07:31.953
not a big thing,

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:35.550
in New York, one of the early auditions

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.640
and it narrowed down
to another girl and me.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.080
- Hmm-mm.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.200
- And I thought I had
it, I thought I had it.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.300
And I said, "Oh, this is it".

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.250
Well I didn't, she got it.

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:51.000
But you know what saved
me from being depressed?

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:51.833
- [Kelly] Yeah?

00:07:51.833 --> 00:07:56.450
- I thought, "Well wait
a minute, it's her turn".

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:58.350
- Oh, wow.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:07:59.520
- It's not my turn.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:00.780
- What a beautiful way to look at it.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.520
- My turn will happen.
- Yeah.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:05.460
- But right now, it's her turn and that,

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.230
it, I give people that advice.

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:08.123
I said, you know what...

00:08:08.123 --> 00:08:08.956
- [Kelly] That's great advice.

00:08:08.956 --> 00:08:10.200
- It just might not be your turn yet.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:11.033
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:08:11.033 --> 00:08:12.870
- But if you have the fire in your belly

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:14.070
and you really wanna do it

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:15.450
and you study hard and everything...

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:16.283
- [Kelly] Keep going.

00:08:16.283 --> 00:08:17.116
- Your turn will come.

00:08:17.116 --> 00:08:18.360
- Yeah, that's great advice.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:20.010
- Yes.
(audience applauds)

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:23.610
- Well, let's turn to more
recent projects right now.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:25.860
You've got a Critics' Choice
Award nomination this year

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:27.090
for "Better Call Saul".

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:29.341
And you're working on a
new series as well, right?

00:08:29.341 --> 00:08:30.174
- Yes.
- What's the new series again?

00:08:30.174 --> 00:08:31.581
- It's called "Palm Royale".

00:08:31.581 --> 00:08:32.414
- [Kelly] "Palm Royale".

00:08:32.414 --> 00:08:37.170
- And it's stars Kristen Wiig,
Allison Janney, Laura Dern.

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:38.100
- What a cast.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:40.110
- Ricky Martin.
- Oh, my gosh.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:42.390
- Oh, so many wonderful people.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:46.800
And it takes place in
Palm Beach in the 1970s.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.350
And it's all about these snooty,

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:54.350
Palm Beach Florida Society
and I'm the matriarch.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:56.340
- Oh, my God, what a fun role.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:57.990
- It is, it is fun.
- Yeah.

00:08:57.990 --> 00:08:59.670
- Do you like playing those kind of roles,

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:00.600
where you kind of get...
- Sure.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:01.980
- Exactly.
- Oh, yeah.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.500
- Yeah, because it's not
your person in real life,

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:05.983
but you do it real well on TV.

00:09:05.983 --> 00:09:07.672
- Thank you.
- It's amazing.

00:09:07.672 --> 00:09:08.505
(Kelly chuckles)

00:09:08.505 --> 00:09:09.780
Well, NBC is celebrating your career

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.430
with a huge tribute tonight.

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:13.050
So, can you describe
the show for everybody

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:13.883
that hasn't seen it?

00:09:13.883 --> 00:09:16.482
- Well, it's...
- Everyone came.

00:09:16.482 --> 00:09:18.750
- Oh, it, it's, I was gobsmacked

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:20.861
by all the people who showed up and came.

00:09:20.861 --> 00:09:21.694
- Yeah.

00:09:21.694 --> 00:09:24.480
- It's like an old time variety show.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.480
We have a 19 piece orchestra.
- Wow.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:30.810
- 19 piece orchestra, live entertainment,

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:32.820
singing with Bernadette Peters,

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.872
Billy Porter, Jane Lynch, Katy Perry.

00:09:35.872 --> 00:09:37.440
- Yeah.
(audience applauds)

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:41.170
- Appearances by Cher and
all of the ladies in...

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:42.030
- [Kelly] Gosh.

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:46.110
- In "Palm Royale", Bob Odenkirk
from "Better Call Saul".

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:46.943
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:09:46.943 --> 00:09:48.210
- I mean, it was just
absolutely incredible.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:50.880
And of course, my chum, Julie Andrews.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:51.723
- [Kelly] Julie was there, yeah.

00:09:51.723 --> 00:09:52.556
(audience applauds)

00:09:52.556 --> 00:09:53.573
- Sitting next to me.
- Yeah.

00:09:53.573 --> 00:09:54.450
(audience applauds)

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.120
- Oh, Lily Thomas, I
mean on and on and on.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:58.550
And just a, a...

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:00.424
- What a perfect way to celebrate.

00:10:00.424 --> 00:10:01.380
- [Carol] Yeah.

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:03.864
- You, because that's how
you like to celebrate life.

00:10:03.864 --> 00:10:05.333
- [Carol] Absolutely, thank you.

00:10:05.333 --> 00:10:06.180
- [Kelly] You know, that's
how you've touched us all,

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:07.230
it's such a perfect way.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:09.870
We have a clip, so here's "Carol Burnett:

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:12.270
90 Years of Laughter and Love".

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:13.440
- When I was outside,

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.390
people were asking me
questions, questions,

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:16.350
questions about you.

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:19.710
And I said, "Oh, she's
kind and she's funny

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:22.080
and she teaches you so much.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:25.680
And she's the kind of woman, kind of star

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.360
that if you meet her, you're
never gonna be disappointed".

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.400
I can always think of, just,

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.530
her being so kind and helping me,

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:38.010
because I wasn't really sure
what I was doing, you know.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:39.420
Because I'd had Sonny as a partner

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:41.850
and when I went on my own,
I was really frightened

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.040
and you helped me and you know, you did.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:45.810
And I don't know, I just love her

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:47.280
and I wanted to be just like her.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:50.343
And I'm not just like you,
but I'm my own version of you.

